Lemma Content and Editor Rules
1. Article Formats

Supported formats:

Article
Investigation
Problem
Essay
Report

The format is metadata and should not determine the fundamental article rendering system.

2. Article Metadata

An article should be able to contain:

title
slug
author
publication date
updated date
description/excerpt
topics
tags
format
reading time
peer-review status
article content
references
images/assets
3. Topics

Primary topics:

Algebra
Calculus
Linear Algebra
Topology
Arithmetic
Statistics & Probability
Number Theory
Computer Science
Numerical Analysis

Use tags for more specific concepts.

4. Mathematical Rendering

Use KaTeX.

Support:

inline equations
block equations

The editor should provide a live preview when practical.

Mathematical rendering must be deterministic and safe.

5. Mathematical Blocks

Support specialized content blocks such as:

Definition
Theorem
Lemma
Proposition
Corollary
Proof
Example
Remark
Exercise

These should have consistent styling.

6. Images and Graphs

V1 supports:

PNG
JPEG/JPG

Authors may generate graphs using Python locally and upload the resulting image.

The editor should allow:

image upload
preview
caption
alt text
appropriate sizing/alignment

Do not implement server-side Python execution for graph generation in V1.

7. Code

Computer-science articles may contain source code.

Code blocks should support:

syntax highlighting
language selection
copy-to-clipboard
responsive scrolling

Common languages should be supported first.

8. Editor

The browser-based editor should eventually provide:

Editor mode
Split mode
Preview mode

Insertion options should include:

text
headings
equations
images
graphs
code
mathematical blocks
tables
references

The author should not need to understand web development.

9. Drafts

Drafts must autosave.

The editor must communicate save status clearly.

Never silently discard unsaved work.

Published content must be protected from accidental direct modification.

10. Review

Articles move through:

DRAFT
→ SUBMITTED
→ UNDER REVIEW
→ REVISION REQUESTED
→ RESUBMITTED
→ APPROVED
→ PUBLISHED

Authors should be able to see the status of their articles.

Reviewers should be able to provide comments and decisions.

11. Public Content

Only approved/published articles should appear in public article discovery.

Drafts and review-stage articles must not accidentally appear in public search results.

12. Content Safety and Integrity

Never allow article content to execute arbitrary JavaScript.

Treat uploaded files and user-generated content as untrusted.

Sanitize rendered content appropriately.

Preserve mathematical notation accurately.

Do not alter the author's mathematical meaning during rendering.